  <h2>Burst vs Sustained: The Economics</h2>
  <h3>Burst Scenario: 4-Hour Refactoring Sprint</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Max 5x:</strong> ~200 Opus messages available. A complex refactor with sub-agents can burn 150–200 messages in 2–3 hours. Throttle likely in hour 3–4.</li>
    <li><strong>Max 20x:</strong> ~800 Opus messages available. Same refactor uses <25% capacity. Zero throttle risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost of throttle:</strong> Engineer waits 30–60 min for capacity recovery. At $100/hr loaded cost, one throttle = $50–100 lost productivity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sustained Scenario: 8-Hour Daily Coding</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Max 5x:</strong> ~400 messages over 8h (with recovery). Comfortable for interactive coding. Throttle only if session density spikes.</li>
    <li><strong>Max 20x:</strong> ~1,600 messages over 8h. Massive headroom. Only multi-agent parallel swarms consume this.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Upgrade Decision Framework</h2>
  <p>Upgrade an engineer from Max 5x → Max 20x <strong>only when all three signals align</strong> for ≥2 consecutive weeks:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Throttle frequency:</strong> ≥3 throttle events/week (not just \"feels slow\" — actual capacity exhaustion)</li>
    <li><strong>API overflow spend:</strong> >$100/mo in Anthropic API charges for that engineer's automation/fallback</li>
    <li><strong>Workload profile:</strong> Documented multi-file refactoring, agentic swarms, or parallel sub-agent tasks</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="background:#101726;padding:12px;border-radius:6px;border-left:4px solid #7AAFFF;color:#E6EAF2;">
    <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> If an engineer doesn't meet all three, Max 5x is the correct tier. Max 20x is a specialization tool, not a status upgrade.
  </p>

